# Goblin — first port from the WAM cast (~/Projects/2026/horse/models/goblin.wam),
# and the first character authored in .blob that was not the zombie.
#
# WAM builds bodies from `loft`s: a profile swept through rings along a bone
# chain. .blob has no loft, so every ring becomes a `blob` at the same relative
# position. That mapping is closer than it sounds — a ring IS an ellipse on a
# bone, which is what a blob is — but two things do not survive it:
#
#   * WAM's `w`/`d` are FULL width and depth; a blob's `r` is a radius. So
#     r = w/2, and deep = d/w to keep the cross-section's aspect.
#   * WAM rings run over a bone CHAIN with t normalised across the whole chain;
#     .blob prims ride ONE bone. Every `at` below was recomputed by walking the
#     chain length. The torso chain is pelvis(0.14) + spine1(0.10) + chest(0.12)
#     = 0.36, so WAM's t=0.58 lands 0.209 along, i.e. spine1 at 0.69.
#
#   (The torso no longer uses this ring mapping: it was rebuilt as three
#   tapered bars on 2026-10-01; see the body block. The chain lengths above
#   are the WAM port's, not this skeleton's.)
#
# Silhouette intent, carried over from the WAM file: stooped and scheming, not
# standing at attention. Spine pitches forward, neck pitches back to bring the
# face level, arms hang long and slightly ahead, legs bow out at the thigh and
# back in at the shin. None of that is animation — it is all rest pose.
#
# GOBLIN-CINEMATIC (goblin expressions Task 13, owner, 2026-10-15: "we would have a base goblin without the facial head
# complexity"). This is the goblin with the FACE RIG: the `jaw` bone with its own `hinge=`, the lower face split onto it at the
# lip line, the mouth interior (the teeth are MESH in its kit since Task 14), the `morph=smile:` words, and the expression clips (expression-library.ts keys
# them `goblin-cinematic`). It is for the cinematic shots (the Flat's screen emergence; PR #47's `goblin-home` can adopt it).
# The base goblin.blob keeps its pre-expression head (f81e5b10) and its cost. Everything below the neck is the base goblin's,
# line for line, and goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts pins that, so the body comments' references to goblin-blob.test.ts hold here
# too. Its kit is goblin-cinematic-kit.wam: the goblin's armour with BARE EYES (no sunglasses), and the teeth.
model goblin-cinematic
  height 1.30
  # humanoid | digitigrade. Checked, not decorative — see blob-checks.ts.
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # ELONGATED, not squat. The first pass put the hip at 0.425 of a 1.15 m body
  # with 0.385 of leg under it, and the result was an egg with stumps: the
  # torso was the whole silhouette and the limbs barely escaped it. Legs are
  # now roughly half the standing height, the torso is longer and much
  # narrower, and every limb segment is a distinct length rather than a blob.
  root pelvis at 0.60 len=0.105

  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=13 len=0.135
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=9  len=0.150
  # No-neck syndrome took four changes, and the non-obvious one was the head.
  # facePrims centres the cranium partway along the skull bone, so an oversized
  # headRadius on a short skull bone puts the bottom of that ellipsoid BELOW
  # the neck's top — the head does not sit near the shoulders, it encloses the
  # whole neck, and thinning the column cannot reveal something inside a
  # sphere. Cranium size, skull length, neck length and the top of the chest (a
  # cap blob until the 2026-10-01 rebuild, now the chest bar's tapered end) all
  # move together; none of them works alone.
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-17 len=0.140
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-5  len=0.235
  # THE JAW (expression pass, 2026-10-13). Not a hinge bone: a CARRIER for one kinematic rig point, exactly as the bride's
  # (bride.blob, bone jaw; jaw.ts). It hangs off the skull's pivot to the chin so gait.ts can name its tail `jaw`. The jaw
  # ROTATES about a hinge on the skull axis (jaw.ts), and rig-bind turns every prim declared `on jaw` by the gape. Rest tail
  # is (0, 1.0875, 0.0898): below and in front of the lower lip.
  # `hinge=0.06` (Task 3b, 2026-10-14): the hinge sits 0.06 along the skull, y 1.1326, ON THE LIP LINE, 2 cm behind the axis. The
  # shared default (jaw.ts JAW_HINGE, 0.30) is the bride's upper lip; on this skull it is 5.6 cm above the lips, and the jaw
  # lever then hangs almost straight down from it, so opening swung the chin back into the throat (at the shout's 0.6 rad the
  # lower jaw vanished into the neck; frames docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/grin/). At the lip line the lower lip
  # drops instead: at 0.5 rad its middle falls 5.1 cm and moves back 1.7 (the shared hinge: 4.7 and 4.5; goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts).
  bone jaw    parent=neck   dir=up pitch=112 len=0.083 hinge=0.06

  mirror
    # WAM's own comment says the shoulder must land INSIDE the chest mass or
    # "the arm floats free". Right for WAM, where the arm is a separate mesh
    # that has to overlap to look attached — and exactly WRONG here, because in
    # an SDF overlapping IS merging and smooth-min swallowed both arms whole.
    # Overshooting is just as wrong: too far out and fusedOf goes POSITIVE, the
    # core-to-core path leaves the flesh and the arm hangs on by nothing. The
    # checks find both edges of a narrow band, and the band MOVES when the
    # torso changes: narrowing the chest to fix the egg silhouette pushed the
    # old shoulder reach outside the flesh and detached both arms outright
    # (fusedOf +0.0076, and validateBody reported the clusters disconnected).
    # Shoulder reach is a function of chest width, not a constant.
    #
    # And "attached" is not the target — DISTINCT is. At side 0.044 + len 0.050
    # the shoulder sat at x 0.094 with a 0.044 nub, against a chest half-width
    # of 0.086: most of the nub was buried and the arms melted into the torso
    # even though every check passed. Fused is a floor, not the goal.
    #
    # Reach alone cannot fix it, though — measured sweep against the ORIGINAL
    # wide torso: 0.046/0.050 left 5 mm of margin, 0.050/0.056 left 0.8 mm,
    # 0.054/0.062 was already detached by fusedOf, and 0.058/0.068 was detached
    # loudly enough that validateBody reported both arms disconnected. There is
    # no reach that both attaches and clears a torso that wide.
    #
    # THE FIX WAS NOT ON THE ARM AT ALL — it was the torso's CROSS-SECTION.
    # Reach, tilt and shoulder radius are all fighting one number: how far out
    # in x the torso's flesh reaches at the height the arm is passing. The arm
    # has to start inside that flesh (or it detaches) and then get outside it
    # (or it melds), so every lever on the arm is trying to win a race the
    # torso sets the length of. Measured on the wide torso, the upper arm's
    # closest approach was 5.4 mm of air at its own midpoint and the forearm's
    # was 18 mm — daylight so thin that at 1.30 m tall neither limb read as
    # separate from any angle, on either the lit or the shadowed side.
    #
    # So the torso gave up width and took it back as DEPTH: every torso ring
    # narrowed in x and grew its `deep` to match, which keeps the creature's
    # bulk (it reads as a pot belly in profile, which is more goblin than the
    # ovoid was) while vacating the exact axis the arms need. That freed the
    # whole budget at once — with the gut at r=0.086 instead of 0.098 a modest
    # tilt of 22 clears it, where 26+ was needed before and read as chicken
    # wings. Measured after: 46 mm at the elbow, 44 mm at the forearm midpoint,
    # 59 mm at the wrist. The clavicle came in to 0.036/0.042 in step, because
    # shoulder reach is a function of chest width and the chest moved.
    # (History: the torso and arms these numbers describe were rebuilt as
    # tapered bars on 2026-10-01, see the body block. The narrow-x, deep-z rule
    # still holds; the arm now measures 48.5 mm of daylight below the elbow.)
    #
    # The knock-on was free: the forearm no longer has to tuck in hard to keep
    # the hands beside the hips, so tilt went -9 to -5, and the hands stopped
    # sitting 12 mm INSIDE the thighs (clear(armL,legL) -0.0121 to +0.0227).
    # The hips narrowed with them (side 0.072 to 0.058, tilt 7 to 5), which the
    # elongated silhouette wanted anyway.
    #
    # characters/goblin-blob.test.ts pins all of this. Arm clearance is not
    # something you can eyeball from the numbers in this file — it is an
    # emergent property of the torso, the clavicle and both arm tilts at once,
    # and it has now silently regressed twice.
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.036 dir=side  len=0.042
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=-12 tilt=22 len=0.235
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=11 tilt=-5 len=0.235
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down             len=0.072
    # Long bowed legs: splay out at the thigh, back in at the shin, so the knee
    # stands proud of the ankle line.
    #
    # PITCH SIGNS ARE FLIPPED FROM THE WAM SOURCE, deliberately. Ported
    # verbatim (thigh pitch=-9, shin pitch=+11) they gave a digitigrade leg —
    # thigh angling BACK and shin forward puts the knee behind the hip-to-ankle
    # line, which reads as a kangaroo hock rather than a knee. In .blob a
    # positive pitch always carries a bone toward +z, so a humanoid knee wants
    # the opposite: thigh FORWARD, shin back, ankle ending up under the hip.
    #
    # This is also the rule the motion work already settled for the zombie —
    # `ik.poleReflect` exists because an unconstrained FABRIK solve folded the
    # knee backwards and kept it, which is the "cow walk" in that code's own
    # comments. A character authored with a backward knee at rest would fight
    # its own IK.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis   dir=down tilt=5  pitch=9  side=0.058 len=0.290
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down tilt=-7 pitch=-11 len=0.270
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd              len=0.130
  end

body
  # Torso: three continuous tapered bars (pelvis, spine, chest), rebuilt 2026-10-01.
  #
  # It used to be five ellipsoids stacked up the spine at blends of 0.004-0.005,
  # and the owner's read was "a series of orbs" (2026-10-01): with blends that
  # tight every ring stays a ring, and the outline pinched between them (a
  # 20.5 mm dip in half-width at the spine/chest joint, between the spine ring
  # and the lower chest ring; goblin-blob.test.ts now pins the deepest dip under
  # 10 mm, and the rebuilt torso's deepest is its 5 mm waist). One bar per bone,
  # tapered with r2 and blended at 0.014-0.016, is one mass: the smooth-min acts
  # over a band about 3 cm wide (smin width = 4 x blend x roundBlendScale 0.5;
  # build-body.ts, validate.ts). The body is the owner's pick of three rendered
  # variants, "A, sinew": docs/dev-notes/2026-10-01-goblin-body-lookdev/.
  #
  # Still narrow in x and deep in z, for the reason the skeleton block gives:
  # width in x is the budget the arms compete for. The chest's wide=1.00 leaves
  # the upper arm 19.2 mm of daylight at 0.11 m (set by the upper-arm bar and
  # the chest width, whatever the shoulder round's size); at the look-dev's
  # wide=1.06 it was 14.99, just under the test's 15 mm line, so the pin
  # failed. Do not widen x or shrink deep here without re-running the arm
  # daylight pins in goblin-blob.test.ts.
  #
  # THE GUT HANGS IN FRONT. `deep=` scales a prim front and back alike, so a deep
  # torso on the spine's own axis bulged at the back of the waist as much as at
  # the belly (the look-dev's flaw). The pelvis and spine bars are pushed
  # forward (+z; offsets are world axes) and the gut is its own blob further
  # forward still, so the back stays flat enough for the spine to show: the
  # front reaches 27 mm further than the back at the belly (pelvis at=0.8) and
  # about 15 mm at the waist (spine1 at=0.3), measured on the bone axis
  # 2026-10-01; goblin-blob.test.ts pins both (over 15 and 8 mm).
  # Measured along the forward-leaning spine bones (spine1 pitch 13, chest 9),
  # not in world profile: there the belly front sits 16 mm behind the chest front
  # and the lower back (the pelvis/spine joint) is the furthest-back point. The
  # owner accepted that profile on 2026-10-01; to make the gut read in profile,
  # pin it in the world frame first (belly front vs chest front), then retune.
  #
  # These bar and gut values are not the look-dev's: they were retuned on
  # 2026-10-01 (goblin refinement phase 1 plan) against the gut measurement
  # above and the arm pins, keeping the look-dev's front silhouette (the back
  # was flattened on purpose, by up to about 28 mm): pelvis r2 0.078 -> 0.074
  # and deep 1.28 -> 1.10; spine r 0.076 -> 0.072 and deep 1.20 -> 1.08; chest
  # deep 1.06 -> 1.12 and wide 1.06 -> 1.00; the gut blob is new.
  # CLOTHES ARE PAINT (owner, 2026-10-02: dark fatigue-grey pants and, under the armour, "a
  # dirty wife-beater undershirt", after the reference photo's soldier). `color=` replaces the
  # flesh albedo wherever that prim is the nearest, so the pelvis (bar + gut blob) is the
  # trousers and the spine1/chest bars and the six vertebra beads are the tank top: nothing to
  # clip, wounds and gibbing still work because the flesh underneath is the same SDF, and
  # removing the paint restores a bare goblin. The boundary sits where the pelvis and spine1
  # bars meet (y 0.705 at rest), which the utility belt covers. Arms are bare, which is what
  # makes it a tank top: the shoulder round and neck stay flesh.
  #
  # DIRT is tone, because paint is one colour per primitive: the chest bar is a dingy off-white
  # (e9e7dd), the spine1 bar below it (the belly, where it would be sweated through and
  # stained) is grimier (cfcab8), and the spine beads darker again (bab5a2) so they read as
  # ridges under cloth, not green bumps through it. The body grain's mottle still rides
  # over paint, so it is not flat. Hexes are eyeballed in the turntable, not measured: the
  # renderer darkens low sRGB values hard (a first 4a4d50 pant read near black), so these are
  # chosen to land on dingy white and fatigue grey on screen (the first cream ddd3b4 rendered as tan skin: the key is warm, so the source is kept NEUTRAL and the light does the yellowing), not to match a paint chip.
  # Pants 7a7e82. Neck, arms, head, hands, ankle knob and feet stay flesh (boots cover the feet).
  # PELVIS AND BUTT, REWORKED 2026-10-03 (owner, seeing the seated pose: "his pelvis dips below when he sits like he has a big
  # diaper ... his pelvis and butt need some work"). The pelvis used to be ONE tapered bar from the root (y 0.60) to the hip joint
  # (y 0.705) with a 68 mm round end cap, so 17 cm of mass hung below the hips. Standing, the thighs hid it; seated, the thighs lie
  # flat forward at hip height (their underside is only 46 mm below the joint) and the pelvis dangled 8 cm under them. Now:
  #   * the pelvis is a squashed sphere AT the hip joint (tall 0.72: its bottom is y 0.648, 57 mm under the joint, about the thigh
  #     underside), so seated it sits flush on the thigh line;
  #   * the gut blob rides at the joint too, 12 mm lower than before and 20 mm further forward, so
  #     the belly keeps its +15 mm over the back (goblin-blob.test.ts) now that the back has a butt;
  #   * a pair of GLUTES (`both`, mirrored in x), 38 mm either side of the spine and 50 mm behind it, at the hip joint's height
  #     minus 3 cm: they are the seat, and from behind they are the cheeks the owner wants, with a 14 mm blend so they merge.
  # The old bar's cap used to fill the crotch between the thighs; the legs now start at a narrow slit (thigh centres 0.116 m
  # apart, r 0.046) under the pelvis, which is how legs meet a body. Measured by eye in the lab, rest and seated (frames in
  # docs/dev-notes/2026-10-03-goblin-poses/).
  blob torso on pelvis at=1.00 r=0.074 tall=0.72 deep=1.10 offset=(0,-0.004,0.012) blend=0.014 color=7a7e82
  blob torso on pelvis at=1.00 r=0.060 tall=1.00 offset=(0,-0.012,0.050) blend=0.016 color=7a7e82
  blob torso on pelvis at=1.00 r=0.050 offset=(0.038,-0.030,-0.046) blend=0.014 both color=7a7e82
  bar  torso on spine1 from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.072 r2=0.068 deep=1.08 offset=(0,0,0.010) blend=0.016 color=cfcab8
  bar  torso on chest  from=0.00 to=0.95 r=0.068 r2=0.056 wide=1.00 deep=1.12 blend=0.016 color=e9e7dd

  # Spine: vertebrae knuckling through the flat back (offsets are world axes;
  # -z is the back), small and tightly blended so each reads as a bump under
  # skin, not as an orb. Seated by measurement (2026-10-01): the chest beads
  # (and the neck one, below) keep the look-dev's offsets, but the three on
  # spine1 were moved onto the pushed-forward bars (the look-dev had -0.086,
  # -0.084, -0.080). Each stands about 5-6 mm proud of the bare back at its
  # height, except the one at the waist (spine1 at=0.55, 8.6 mm), where the
  # back curves in and the spine should show.
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.15 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.072) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.55 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.066) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.95 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.072) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2
  blob torso on chest  at=0.30 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.068) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2
  blob torso on chest  at=0.65 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.062) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2
  blob torso on chest  at=0.98 r=0.012 offset=(0,0,-0.056) blend=0.005 color=bab5a2

  # Neck: one tapered column (0.040 at the collar to 0.033 under the skull), a
  # pair of cords from the collarbone notch up the front of the neck to the
  # base of the skull, converging (the sinew that makes a thin neck read as a
  # neck rather than a pipe), and one more vertebra. Values from the look-dev
  # variant A, owner-picked.
  bar  head on neck from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.040 r2=0.033 blend=0.010
  blob head on neck at=0.05 r=0.009 r2=0.007 offset=(0.026,0.000,0.018) tip=(-0.012,0.120,-0.030) blend=0.006 both
  blob head on neck at=0.30 r=0.010 offset=(0,0,-0.036) blend=0.005

  # Ears: ONE tapered prim each, swept out and back to a point.
  #
  # This used to be two blobs apiece, and the comment here said why: "one
  # ellipsoid cannot taper — it is round whatever you scale it to — so each ear
  # is a flattened base plus a much smaller tip pushed further out and up."
  # That is a description of the format's limit, not of an ear. The smooth-min
  # between the two prims was doing the tapering, which meant the taper was a
  # FILLET — convex, bulging out before it came back in — and the tip was still
  # a small sphere. Mickey Mouse, which is what the owner called it.
  #
  # `r2=0.003` is a real point, and `tip=` sweeps it out and up out of a skull
  # bone that runs straight up. `chamfer` creases the ear into the head instead
  # of letting it melt in. The skull's own half-width is headRadius x headWidth
  # = 0.121, so the base at x 0.086 sits INSIDE the cranium (which is what
  # attaches it) and the point stands 52 mm clear.
  blob head on skull at=0.58 r=0.036 r2=0.003 wide=0.80 deep=0.44 blend=0.0020 chamfer offset=(0.086,0.006,-0.006) tip=(0.087,0.104,-0.022) both

  # Nose — A REAL HOOK, ONE PRIMITIVE.
  #
  # What was here: two tapered prims — a bridge running down and out from
  # between the eyes, then a smaller hooked tip turning down off its end. That
  # was itself an upgrade over the ORIGINAL two ellipsoids faked into a hook
  # (which only read from the front), but it was still a chain: the joint
  # between bridge and hook carried the bridge's own round base, and under the
  # smooth-min the two segments read as a sausage with a kink rather than one
  # curved nose.
  #
  # `bend=` makes the whole thing ONE primitive: a capsule swept along a
  # quadratic Bezier from the bridge's start to the hook's point, control
  # point pushed FORWARD of the chord so the curve goes out along the old
  # bridge direction and then turns down — the bend is the goblin, and the
  # round base at the old joint is gone entirely. The radius lerps along the
  # CURVE now, and at the old joint (t ≈ 0.57) it reads 10 mm — the old hook
  # prim's radius, so the silhouette keeps its slenderness through the turn.
  #
  # Aiming the bend: the chord midpoint sits at (0,-0.043,0.116) relative to
  # the anchor; the control point wants to be where the bridge and hook
  # TANGENTS intersect, at (0,-0.041,0.143) — so the displacement from the
  # midpoint is (0, +0.002, +0.027). The apex bulges halfway out, ~13 mm
  # forward of the chord, which is the hook.
  #
  # REACH still rules: the point sits at z=0.162, 44 mm proud of the
  # cranium's 0.118 m semi-depth. SLENDER still rules: r=0.019 at the base
  # (a fat base read as a bulb wearing a spike), r2=0.003 at the point.
  blob head on skull at=0.47 r=0.019 r2=0.003 wide=0.80 blend=0.0016 chamfer offset=(0,-0.010,0.070) tip=(0,-0.066,0.092) bend=(0,0.002,0.027) morph=smile:(0,0.006,0.002)tip(0,0.006,0)

  # BIG LIPS — a protruding muzzle, the cartoon-mouse read.
  #
  # The face SHEET already draws a mouth, but a painted mouth is flat: it
  # cannot break the silhouette, so from any angle off dead-front the goblin
  # had a smooth jaw with a dark smear on it. Lips are the one facial feature
  # that has to be geometry, because the whole point of them is that they stick
  # out.
  #
  # Two prims, creased against each other with `chamfer` rather than blended:
  # a fillet between an upper and lower lip fuses them into one sausage, and
  # the SEAM is what makes it read as a mouth. `wide` well above 1 spreads them
  # across the face; `tall` well under 1 keeps each one a flat band rather than
  # a ball.
  #
  # Sitting below the nose (skull at=0.47) and above the jaw, so all three
  # features stack instead of competing for the same flesh.
  # REACH, again: the cranium's semi-depth is 0.118, so lips whose front edge
  # lands short of that are a texture with extra steps. These reach 0.130 —
  # 12 mm proud, and deliberately no more.
  #
  # THE NOSE HAS TO STAY THE FACE'S LEAD. A first pass at r=0.040 / wide=1.70
  # reached 0.142 and spread most of the way across the head, and the goblin
  # turned into a cartoon mouse: the lips became the muzzle and the hooked nose
  # read as part of it. The nose tips out at 0.162, so keeping the lips a clear
  # 30 mm behind it is what preserves the hook as the silhouette's leading
  # edge. Lips, not a snout.
  #
  # AND THEY HAVE TO SIT BELOW THE NOSE, which is not automatic. At skull 0.31
  # the upper lip landed at y=1.194 and the nose tip at y=1.1935 — the same
  # height — so smooth-min fused them into one broad snout with a spike on the
  # front. The owner read it as "a big broad disk intersecting the nose", and
  # that is exactly what two features competing for the same flesh looks like.
  # Dropped to 0.12 — 5.6 cm of face between the nose tip and the upper lip.
  # 0.23 was a first attempt and STILL merged: 2 cm is inside the reach of the
  # nose's own blend plus the lips', so the two masses found each other anyway.
  # Separation has to beat the sum of the blends, not just be nonzero.
  #
  # The jaw grew to make the room (jawDrop 0.072 to 0.104). Lengthening the
  # lower face is the honest fix: cramming three features into a short muzzle
  # is what forced them to overlap, and shrinking the lips instead just makes
  # a goblin with no mouth.
  #
  # Their reach came down with the move. The cranium is an ellipsoid, so its
  # semi-depth SHRINKS as you go lower — 0.108 at this height, not the 0.118 at
  # the nose — and lips sized against the wrong number stick out too far.
  blob head on skull at=0.12 r=0.028 r2=0.021 wide=1.25 tall=0.44 blend=0.0020 chamfer offset=(0,0.006,0.062) tip=(0,-0.006,0.044) morph=smile:(0,0.014,-0.006)*(1.1,0.8,0.9) morph=gape:(0,0.005,0) morph=sneer:(0,0.008,-0.004)
  # THE MOUTH OPENS AT THE LIP LINE (Task 3b, 2026-10-14; owner: "the main thing is getting a viable toothy grin with teeth").
  # Task 2's split left the skull-side mass hanging to y 1.10 at the front, so the opening appeared in the chin's bevel 2-3 cm
  # under the lips and the teeth hid behind skin. Now the SEAM is the lip line, y ~1.133: everything above it is the skull's
  # (the shortened face-block jaw ellipsoid, the upper lip, the upper ridge below) and everything under it is the jaw's (the
  # lower lip, the chin, the corners, the floor). Rest front within 2.5 mm of the approved profile except at y 1.13, where the
  # groove used to cut (goblin-head-profile.ts says why that pin moved).
  #
  # Lower lip: a BENT bar along the dental arch (was a front-to-back pout capsule whose front sloped down to y 1.10: it was the
  # chin's shelf, not a lip). Top y 1.1335, meeting the upper ridge's underside; front z 0.122, 1.5 cm behind the upper lip's
  # (an overbite). Flesh, about the old lip's size; no colour (the owner does not want painted lips).
  blob head on jaw at=1.0 r=0.010 tall=0.90 blend=0.0016 chamfer offset=(-0.026,0.037,0.0102) tip=(0.052,0,0) bend=(0,0,0.024) morph=smile:(-0.0391,0.0289,-0.0192)tip(0.0782,0,0)bend(0,-0.1018,0.0377)*0.75 morph=gape:(0,-0.015,0.010)
  # THE CHIN: the lower part of what used to be the jaw ellipsoid. Centre (0, 1.1025, 0.053), semi-axes 0.061 x 0.026 x 0.058.
  # Its top (1.128 on the axis) is LOWER than Task 2's (1.137) and must stay under the floor's top (below): once the jaw tips
  # open, a chin top above the floor shows as a lit band of skin inside the mouth (it did, at 1.131). Its front makes the chin's
  # reach (y 1.09), its underside the chin's underside; both are profile pins, so retune it with the CLI.
  # TASK 21 (owner: "there's still this like disk neck throat bulge thing going on that i would like to smooth out/remove"): this
  # flattened ellipsoid WAS the disc. A CPU marker render (each prim its own colour) put its flat underside straight onto the thin
  # neck column (`bar head on neck`, r 0.033 under the skull): a 12 cm wide puck on a 6.6 cm stick, the concave corner between them
  # in shadow (the dark rim; it shows at neutral, gape 0, so it is geometry and shading, not the jaw's noise-anchor seam). Now 6%
  # narrower (wide 1.33 -> 1.25, x semi-axis 0.0577) and the THROAT FILL below tapers it into the neck.
  blob head on jaw at=1.0 r=0.0462 wide=1.25 tall=0.56 deep=1.25 blend=0.010 offset=(0,0.0150,-0.0368) morph=smile:(0,-0.004,0.004)*(1.1,1.1,0.85)
  # THE THROAT FILL (Task 21; jaw): a tapered blob from inside the chin's underside (centre (0, 1.0855, 0.0528), r 32 mm, x1.5 wide)
  # down and back to inside the neck's front (r 16 mm at (0, 1.0655, 0.0358)), blend 12 mm: the chin's underside becomes a cone
  # into the throat instead of a flat shelf. The front at x 0 now slopes 0.104 (y 1.09) -> 0.094 (1.08) -> 0.085 (1.07) -> 0.077
  # (1.055, the neck) where it dropped 0.104 -> 0.082 -> 0.069 in 2 cm; the half-width tapers 0.060 -> 0.041 over 1.09-1.06 instead
  # of 0.054 -> 0.037 inside 1.5 cm. Its lower end is buried in the neck (front 0.052 against the neck's 0.068), so the neck below
  # y 1.04 (within 0.5 mm) and the armour collar are untouched. On the JAW so it opens with the chin (a skull/neck fill would stay behind as a
  # bulge when the jaw drops); a wide gape swings its lower end back and up into the neck. It follows the chin's smile (down 4 mm,
  # 10% wider). The jaw's noise seam (Task 16) now falls where the fill meets the neck (y ~1.05) instead of the chin's rim.
  blob head on jaw at=1.0 r=0.032 r2=0.016 wide=1.5 blend=0.012 offset=(0,-0.002,-0.037) tip=(0,-0.020,-0.017) morph=smile:(0,-0.004,0.003)*(1.1,1,1)

  # UPPER RIDGE (the upper gum, skull): a flat bent bar along the arch, x +-0.045, y 1.133-1.145, front z 0.117 at the middle.
  # The shortened jaw ellipsoid recedes to z ~0.10 just above the seam; without this the sides of the face between the upper
  # lip and the seam were 1 cm deep and the tooth roots showed through at rest. Its flat underside is the mouth's top edge:
  # the upper teeth hang from it.
  blob head on skull at=0.0 r=0.011 tall=0.55 blend=0.002 offset=(-0.034,0.0204,0.0811) tip=(0.068,0,0) bend=(0,0,0.024) morph=smile:(-0.0255,0.0262,-0.0227)tip(0.0511,0,0)bend(0,-0.0333,0.0443)*(1,0.8,1) morph=gape:(0,0.006,0) morph=sneer:(0,0.004,-0.001)tip(0,0.010,0)
  # CHEEK FILL (skull, both sides), at x 0.046, y 1.122: the jaw/skull seam wraps round the side of the face and left a 4 mm
  # waist in the front silhouette at y 1.12. This fills it (silhouette back within 1 mm of the approved head at every height).
  blob head on skull at=0.0 r=0.012 blend=0.006 offset=(0.046,0.0034,0.0271) both morph=smile:(0.0149,0.0162,0.0287)*1.1blend*2.5
  # THE CHEEK ROLLS (Task 18, the cartoon grin; skull, both sides): a bent bar along the nasolabial line, from beside the nose wing
  # (x 0.024, y 1.17) down and out past the mouth corner. At rest it is a 3 mm thread buried 2-4 cm inside the cheek mass and the
  # cheek fill (goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts: every point at least 1 mm under the rest flesh; the head-profile CLI unchanged). On
  # the smile it comes 2.6 cm forward and swings its far end out and down, bows out, and grows 1.7x (r 5.1 mm; 2.4x until Task 20's
  # de-chipmunk): a roll framing the
  # grin from the nose to below the corner, its small blend (1.5 mm) leaving a crease where it meets the cheek. TONED DOWN (owner,
  # 2026-10-15, on the first cartoon clip: "the rolls are too puffy ... those jowls"): it was 4.8 cm forward and 4.5x (r 13.5 mm),
  # and with the cheek fill's x1.6 the lower face read as a ring of jowls; cartoon/rolls-toned-down.png is the after.
  # The reference's strongest single cue. Two prims (the plan allowed four): one roll reads from the front and at 3/4; a jaw-side
  # second roll round the chin was not needed once the lower lip itself became the fat lower band. CORNER FIX (below, "TASK 18
  # FOLLOW-UP"): it now bows OUT round the mouth corner like a "(" (passing ~1 cm outside it) and ends below and outside it, so
  # the corner tucks under it; it used to cross BEHIND the lip's end, which then hooked up past it.
  blob head on skull at=0.0 r=0.003 blend=0.0015 offset=(0.024,0.0494,0.0671) tip=(0.024,-0.044,-0.038) bend=(0.004,0.004,0) both morph=smile:(0.004,0.0029,0.026)tip(0.0176,0.0048,0.0083)bend(0.0412,0.0047,-0.0029)*1.7blend*1.5 morph=sneer:(0,0.008,0.002)tip(0,0.004,0)
  # THE FURROW (Task 19, the evil grin; owner: "a variant like an 'evil grin' where the brows angle more inward to produce a furrow";
  # skull, both sides): a short upright bar at each inner brow head (the corrugator bunching), from the V's knot up and a little in.
  # At rest a 2.8 mm thread buried 6 mm inside the glabella (goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts: every point at least 1 mm under the rest
  # flesh); on `furrow` it comes forward to stand proud of the dropped brow ridge (browDown) and fattens, its small blend leaving a
  # crease where the pair meet at the midline and where each meets the ridge: the furrow is the valley between two bulges, not a cut.
  # TASK 20 (push the evil grin further): further forward and down, 2.1x (was 1.8x), as the painted creases went to half strength.
  blob head on skull at=0.0 r=0.0028 blend=0.0012 offset=(0.012,0.1204,0.0871) tip=(-0.001,0.014,0) both morph=furrow:(-0.0015,-0.003,0.0275)*2.1
  # MOUTH CORNERS (jaw, both sides), at x 0.037, y 1.124: the lowered chin left the corners open at rest (the throat bar showed
  # there). They drop with the jaw, so they never plug the opening.
  blob head on jaw at=1.0 r=0.010 blend=0.004 offset=(0.037,0.0365,-0.0048) both morph=smile:(0.0256,0.0504,-0.0114)*0.9blend*3 morph=sneer:(0,0.010,-0.002)
  # THE FLOOR (jaw): a dark flat disc behind the lower lip, top y 1.131, z 0.046-0.106, the lower teeth rooted in it. With the
  # hinge on the lip line the opening jaw TIPS its top toward the viewer, and without a floor the chin's top showed through the
  # open mouth as lit green skin. Dark like the throat bar and buried at rest the same way (goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts).
  blob head on jaw at=1.0 r=0.030 wide=0.88 tall=0.30 deep=1.6 blend=0.002 offset=(0,0.0365,-0.0318) color=2a0608 gloss=0.05 morph=smile:(0,-0.020,-0.024) morph=gape:(0,-0.012,-0.010)

  # THE SMILE MOVES THE FACE (Task 12, 2026-10-15; owner: "when smiling i feel his face geometry isnt changing enough like his
  # chins n stuff should get wider"). The `morph=smile:` words above move existing prims by the expression's smile weight
  # (morph.ts; rig-bind applyRig moves the REST geometry before the jaw opens and the head turns); at smile 0 nothing moves and
  # the head packs bit-identically. No prim was added. Tuned by eye on the grin close-up (docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-
  # expressions/morphs/, which-prim.png colours each one):
  #  - LOWER LIP: ends out 6 mm (tip 12 mm) and back 5 mm, the middle sagging 7 mm under them (bend y -14 mm; bend z +10 mm keeps
  #    the middle where it was in depth), thinned to 0.85: the opening becomes a U whose corners turn up. This is the smile.
  #  - MOUTH CORNERS: up 8 mm and back 8 mm, shrunk to 0.75. Pushed OUT (the first try) they read as two balls beside the mouth at
  #    45 degrees; shrunk to 0.6 the side notch reopened. 0.75 closes it; a small knob with a crease is left (the lower lip's
  #    chamfer), which reads as the corner bunching.
  #  - CHIN: 12% wider (the x semi-axis 0.0614 -> 0.0688) and 3 mm lower: the lower face is ~1 cm a side wider at y 1.07-1.08.
  #  - CHEEK FILL: up 7 mm, out 3, forward 1, radius x1.15: the cheeks bunch over the corners (a visible bump at 45 degrees).
  #  - UPPER RIDGE: ends up 2 mm and out 4 (tip 8), the middle down 1 (bend): the upper edge follows the smile line.
  #  - UPPER LIP: up 2 mm, back 1, 8% wider.
  # gape morphs to round the shout's box were tried and dropped (morphs/shout-gape-try.png: the corners swallowed two lower teeth
  # and the grin's opening turned into a V).
  # TASK 15 (2026-10-15; owner on the grin clip: "it actually reads more like surprise"): the grin's jaw now barely opens (0.12 rad,
  # was 0.204), so the WIDTH comes from the smile morphs and the eyes do the rest (the sheet's squint, the face block's morph lines).
  # Widened by frames (docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/smile/): the opening's marker width went 198 -> 236 px.
  #  - LOWER LIP: ends out 17 mm (was 6; tip 34), back 7, up 1; the middle sags 4.5 mm under them (bend -9, was -14: the deep U
  #    read as a dropped jaw); radius x0.88. Thinned to 0.72 it SPLIT in the middle (the floor showed through as a notch).
  #  - MOUTH CORNERS: out 13 mm, up 4, back 9 (was up 8, out 2), x0.65: up 8 they plugged the opening's ends.
  #  - CHEEK FILL: out 6, up 9, forward 3, x1.2 (was 3/7/1, x1.15). UPPER RIDGE: ends out 13 (tip 26), up 4, middle down 1.
  #  - THROAT BAR: ends out 8 mm each (it is what shows dark at the widened corners).
  # TASK 16 (owner, screenshot 2: "some weird shape stuff happening in the grin"). Marker frames (each prim painted its own colour)
  # put names on it: the round KNOB at the mouth corner was the corner pair (a 6.5 mm ball pushed 13 mm out to the lower lip's
  # end, blend 0.004), the separate CHEEK BUMP was the cheek fill (a 14 mm ball pushed up 9 mm, blend 0.006), and the dark crease
  # lines were NOT geometry (the CPU field is smooth there): they were the skin noise's rest-space anchor jumping at the edge of
  # every morphed prim (fixed in rig-bind.ts morphedRest, used by the lab's upload). Now both pairs MELT on the smile (morph.ts
  # `blend*k`) instead of sitting on the face as balls:
  #  - MOUTH CORNERS: out 12, up 4, back 8 (was 13/4/9), x0.85 (was 0.65: a melted corner needs the volume to fill the corner),
  #    blend x3.
  #  - CHEEK FILL: out 6, up 6, forward 2 (was up 9, forward 3: it rose clear of the seam it fills), x1.25, blend x2.5.
  # TASK 18 (owner: "when he grins it should be a bit cartoonish and exaggerated", a 3D emoji evil grin as the look reference):
  # centimetres, not millimetres. The grin clip also squashes the whole head (squash 1: 15% wider, 8% shorter, squash.ts) and
  # opens the jaw to 0.18 rad, which DROPS everything on the jaw 1-2 cm at the front, so the jaw-side moves below overshoot it.
  # Tuned by eye beside the reference over eight capture rounds (docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/cartoon/):
  #  - LOWER LIP: ends out 5.2 cm each (tip 10.4), UP 4.4 cm, back 2.6; the middle sags under them (bend -11.5 cm: a deep U whose
  #    arms reach the cheek rolls), x1.1 thicker: the fat lower band of the crescent. End to end 2.8x the neutral's.
  #  - UPPER RIDGE: ends out 3.8 cm and up 3.6 (the middle stays: bend -7.2), back 2: the crescent's top edge curving up.
  #  - THROAT BAR: ends out 3.6, up 2.6 and back 1.2, bowed, 30% taller: the dark of the crescent's arms. (Out 5 put its ends
  #    beyond the face's side: a dark slit showed in profile.)
  #  - MOUTH CORNERS: out 4.2, up 4.4, to the lower lip's ends. CHIN: 40% wider and 25% taller, 6 mm lower (higher, its top
  #    showed as lit skin inside the opening). UPPER LIP: 40% wider. NOSE (the hook, above): up 6 mm and its point 6 mm shorter.
  #  - CHEEK FILL: out 1.6, up 1.2, forward 0.8, x1.25 (was out 2.4, up 1.8, forward 1.2, x1.6 until the owner called the jowls): it plugs the side of the face behind the cheek roll (without it the open
  #    corner showed as a dark pocket from the side).
  # TASK 18 FOLLOW-UP, THE CORNER (owner, on the grin's mouth corner at three-quarter: "this area still looks quite messed up").
  # Marker frames (each prim its own colour, CPU and GPU) named it: the lower lip's ENDS sat at x +-0.090 (squashed), 4.4 cm up
  # and 2.6 cm back, i.e. OUTSIDE the face's side (the head without the mouth prims ends at x ~0.08 there): the fat band hooked
  # up and round the side like a beak; the corner pair followed it out there; the cheek roll's far end crossed BEHIND the lip's
  # end (two lumps), and the throat bar's ends (x +-0.075) reached the side of the face, so its dark showed through the gap
  # between the lip's end, the roll and the cheek fill (the hole). The numbers below were SOLVED for posed targets at the grin's
  # peak (smile 1, squash 1, gape 0.15 rad; positions after the squash), the morph words are their inverse:
  #  - LOWER LIP: ends at (+-0.070, 1.160, 0.092) (were +-0.090, 1.161, 0.082), the middle at (0, 1.102, 0.116): the U ends on
  #    the face's front, where the cheek roll caps it. End to end 2.30x the neutral's (was 2.84; the pin asks 1.8).
  #  - UPPER RIDGE: ends at (+-0.068, 1.174, 0.076), 6 mm lower and inside the cheek mass (they were poking out above the roll).
  #  - MOUTH CORNERS: at (0.072, 1.166, 0.082), just behind where the lip and the ridge meet: they plug the crescent's end from
  #    inside and the side, and are never the surface you see.
  #  - CHEEK ROLL: same nose end, bows out round the corner (passing ~(0.082, 1.162, 0.094)) to (0.080, 1.140, 0.080); blend x1.5.
  #  - CHEEK FILL: at (0.070, 1.146, 0.072), x1.4 (was (0.071, 1.142, 0.050), x1.25): it backs the roll and the corner, so
  #    nothing behind them is open.
  #  - THROAT BAR: ends at x +-0.056 (were 0.075), inside the crescent, never at the side.
  #  - UPPER LIP: up 6 mm (was 3): the narrower crescent keeps its height at the front.
  #  - CHIN: up 2 mm and forward 4 (was up 6, back 6), 20% taller and 15% shallower (was 5% shorter): a rounder jaw that slopes
  #    into the neck instead of a flat shelf over it; still 15% wider (+9.5 / +9.0 mm a side at y 1.10 / 1.11).
  # Rest unchanged (morph words only: weights 0 pack bit-identically, the head-profile CLI prints the same). Frames:
  # docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/cartoon/corner-before-after.png, grin-corner-fix.mp4.
  # TASK 20 (owner on PR #48: "when he grins there's this dark gap - i guess its because his bottom teeth dont cover that space ...
  # cartoonishly his teeth would be bigger and take up the space ... there's something a little too chipmunky about his overall
  # grin"; "can you push the evil grin further"). The kit's teeth are now TWO BIG ROWS (8 upper, 8 lower; goblin_teeth.py), laid out
  # where this grin puts the lips, and the grin opens with the LIPS: the clip's gape is 0.12 (0.072 rad, was 0.25 = 0.15 rad) and
  # its squash 0.5 (was 1). Solved for posed targets at the grin's peak as in Task 18's follow-up:
  #  - UPPER RIDGE: middle at (0, 1.152, 0.108) (was 1.143: lifted 9 mm off the upper row), ends at (+-0.064, 1.168, 0.073); 20%
  #    thinner on the smile, so its shadowed underside is a thin rim over the teeth, not a band.
  #  - LOWER LIP: pulled DOWN off the lower row, middle at (0, 1.099, 0.112) (its top 1.109, the lower row standing on it), ends at
  #    (+-0.070, 1.150, 0.084) (were 1.165: a shallower U, so its arms no longer cover the side teeth), radius x0.75 (was x1.1: the
  #    fat lower lip that hid the lower row).
  #  - UPPER LIP: up 14 mm and back 6 (was 6 / 1), 10% wider (was 40%), 20% flatter: it gets out of the way instead of overhanging.
  #  - THE FLOOR (dark, jaw): down 20 mm and back 24 on the smile: its front used to stand in front of the lower row and hid it
  #    (and at 12 mm down a sliver of it still showed through the seam between the lower lip and the chin, a dark line under the teeth).
  #  - THROAT BAR: ends at x +-0.041 (were 0.052): the narrower cheek fill no longer covered them at three-quarter (a dark slit).
  #  - CHIN: 10% wider (was 15%) and 4 mm DOWN (was up 2), so its top stays under the lower lip.
  # LESS CHIPMUNK (the puff, not the crease): the cheek fill x1.1 (was x1.4), the cheek rolls x1.7 (was x2.4), the face block's
  # cheek mass 3% NARROWER on the smile (was 6% wider), the squash halved (above).
  # THE GAPE (shout, scowl, surprise): the lower lip drops 15 mm and comes 10 mm forward per unit gape, the floor drops behind the
  # lower row, the ridge and the upper lip lift 6 / 5 mm, so a wide-open mouth shows both rows too (the rows are laid out for the
  # grin, higher in the jaw than Task 17's).
  # THE SNEER (the evil grin's own mouth, expression.ts `sneer`): the upper ridge lifts LOPSIDED (+4 mm at the goblin's right end,
  # +14 mm at its left: the left fang bared), the upper lip lifts 8 mm, the corners and the cheek rolls hike 8-10 mm.

  # NO GROOVE ANY MORE (Task 3b). A groove at y 1.1327 drew the mouth line from 2026-08 to Task 3b, but a groove cuts 4 mm of
  # FIELD from every prim in the head cluster, teeth included: once the jaw opened, it bit the upper teeth in half right where
  # they show (sdBody +1.1 mm on a tooth's axis). The line is now the real seam between the lips plus the painted sheet mouth.

  # THE MOUTH'S INSIDE (expression pass, 2026-10-13; owner: "a toothy evil grin", jagged and uneven with two fangs). The teeth
  # moved to the kit in Task 14 (below). The inside is buried when the jaw is shut (goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts pins both ends
  # inside the rest of the flesh) and shows only as the gape drops the jaw side away from the skull side at the lip line (Task 3b).
  #
  # Three traps found tuning the SDF teeth (history since Task 14; each would have passed the endpoint test):
  #  - BLEND. The plan's `blend=0.002 chamfer` bridges anything within 4k = 8 mm, and the teeth fold BEFORE the face
  #    block's prims (body-block head prims always come first in the head cluster): they chamfered into the lips and chin
  #    and grew skin up to 4 mm out through the rest face. Teeth are hard: `blend=0.0002`, no chamfer.
  #  - THE GROOVE (since removed, see above). It cut 4 mm of FIELD, teeth included.
  #  - PAINT. Paint is the hit's nearest prim (validate.ts nearestPrim). The skin at the corners (x 0.03-0.036,
  #    y 1.106-1.114) is a blend fillet with every real prim 5-8 mm away, so a lower-corner tooth root 5 mm off painted it
  #    ivory at rest. The corner lower teeth lean forward like small tusks, roots 6 mm further back.
  # Checked with a rest-skin paint sweep (no tooth or interior prim paints at gape 0).
  #
  # The inside is a dark bar on the skull (the bride's trick, bride.blob), the throat: x +-0.041, y 1.090-1.138, z 0.060-0.084,
  # 4-6 cm behind the lips' front. Never the nearest prim to the rest skin, so it paints nothing at rest; it is what shows
  # between and behind the upper teeth (mesh, the kit) from the first millimetre of gape. Task 3b moved it up and forward to the new seam,
  # made it tall enough to meet the floor (a gap between the two showed a lit band of skin), and took its gloss down (0.85 lit
  # the bar's ends as grey edges framing the mouth).
  bar  head on skull from=0.0 to=0.0 offset=(-0.029,-0.0046,0.0591) tip=(0.058,0,0) r=0.012 tall=2.0 blend=0.003 color=2a0608 gloss=0.05 morph=smile:(-0.0091,0.0286,-0.009)tip(0.0183,0,0)bend(0,-0.0565,0.0248)*(1,1.3,1)
  #
  # THE TEETH ARE MESH NOW (Task 14, 2026-10-15; owner: "lets do the mesh teeth, only in the cinematic variant"). The eleven
  # tooth prims that stood here (5 small upper and 2 fangs on the skull, 4 small lower on the jaw; `color=e0d8c0 gloss=0.35
  # glow=0.15` from Task 12) cost about 23 ms on a face close-up, buried at rest, and were hard to light inside the cavity. They are
  # polygon sweeps in goblin-cinematic-kit.wam at the same roots, lengths and leans (that file's TEETH block), skinned to `skull`
  # and `jaw`; rig-bind.ts `kitFrames` gives the kit's jaw bone the `on jaw` prims' own transform so the lower row stays on the
  # floor and behind the lower lip. The throat bar above and the floor (`on jaw`, further up) stay SDF: they are the dark mouth the
  # mesh teeth are seen against, and the SDF's depth is what hides the teeth while the jaw is shut (the kit depth-tests against
  # the raymarched flesh). What the old comments said about the rows' layout lives on in the kit's TEETH block; the three traps
  # above (blend, groove, paint) were SDF-tooth problems and are history now. Since Task 17 the teeth are sculpted in Blender
  # (assets-source/goblin-teeth/goblin_teeth.py, merged by scripts/merge-kit-teeth.mjs), since Task 20 two rows of 8 laid out for
  # the grin's lips (the mouth morphs above): change those and the generator's GRIN_* constants and lip curves must follow.

  # ARMS AND LEGS: tapered bars, no ball joints (rebuilt 2026-10-01).
  #
  # Most joints used to be a nub 1.3-1.7x the shaft it joined (wrist and ankle
  # about 1.1x), at blends of 0.0014-0.004, on purpose: "shoulder, elbow,
  # wrist, hip, knee, ankle" as orbs. The owner's 2026-10-01 read was "a series
  # of orbs", and the pick was variant A: each segment one bar tapering toward
  # the hand or foot (r2), blended into the next at 0.010-0.014 (a smooth-min
  # band 2-3 cm wide; see the torso block) so a joint is a soft transition. The
  # orbs that stay are kept on purpose: the owner kept the hands as orbs, a
  # throwback to early 3D games, and the small ankle knob and the toe pad come
  # with variant A; the shoulder round is also the arm's core (below).
  # goblin-blob.test.ts pins that no other point-blob comes back on a limb. All
  # values below are look-dev variant A's.
  #
  # The shoulder round is the arm's `core`: the prim the fuse probe starts
  # from (clusterCore in validate.ts). Without the mark the probe takes the
  # cluster's fattest prim, and the hand (0.046 x deep 0.90 = 0.0414)
  # out-ranks a slim shoulder: the probe then ran from the hand to the torso
  # through air and fusedOf read +3.4 mm, though nothing had detached
  # (daylight and validateBody were unchanged). Marked `core`, the look-dev's
  # r 0.038 fuses at -2.7 mm. Do not "fix" a positive fusedOf by growing this
  # orb; check which prim is the core first.
  blob arm on clavicle at=1.00 r=0.038 blend=0.010 mirror core
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.031 r2=0.024 blend=0.010 mirror
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.027 r2=0.018 blend=0.010 mirror

  # The hand is no longer flesh: the bony long-fingered hand (owner,
  # 2026-10-24) is polygon kit, merged into goblin-kit.gltf on the hand bone by
  # scripts/merge-kit-hands.ts from the FPV arm's own hand mesh. What stays
  # here is a WRIST KNOB the kit palm sits into, so the forearm still ends in
  # a round (the early-3D accent) and there is no seam where mesh meets SDF.
  # It replaced the r 0.046 orb at at=0.55; GOBLIN_SKIN.handRadius keeps the
  # old 0.046 for loadHold's shell offsets, no longer this prim's radius.
  blob arm on hand at=0.10 r=0.024 blend=0.008 mirror

  # The legs wear the trousers' paint (see the torso block). The shin bar runs
  # to the ankle, so the boot in goblin-kit.wam covers the seam.
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.046 r2=0.031 blend=0.014 mirror color=7a7e82
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.034 r2=0.021 blend=0.012 mirror color=7a7e82
  # The ankle knob: small, kept as the early-3D accent where shin meets foot.
  blob leg on shin  at=1.00 r=0.026 blend=0.008 mirror

  # NO FLESH FOOT while the goblin wears the kit's boots (owner, 2026-10-02:
  # "the boots clip"; the soldier made the same call on 2026-09-05). The boot
  # IS the foot. In the walk pose the flesh foot swung out from under the boot
  # sole (frames in docs/dev-notes/2026-10-02-goblin-armour/), because the rig
  # moves the foot's SDF prims and the kit's rigid skin slightly differently
  # (the known 8-10 cm foot stretch, refinement spec phase 4). The foot BONE
  # stays: the boot is skinned to it. For a barefoot goblin, uncomment these
  # two lines (they are the phase-1 body's feet, unchanged):
  # Feet: a shaft forward along the foot bone and a toe pad, slimmed to the new
  # shin (they were sized for the old 0.035 shin and 0.038 ankle nub). Splayed
  # and a little oversized for the leg, which is what stops a long-legged
  # creature reading as stilts. Values from the look-dev variant A.
  # bar  leg on foot  from=0.05 to=0.80 r=0.028 r2=0.024 tall=0.80 blend=0.010 mirror
  # blob leg on foot  at=0.92 r=0.034 wide=1.15 tall=0.70 deep=0.95 blend=0.008 mirror

# Wound pass r2: hidden bone, revealed by a wound — same two authored shapes
# as the zombie, scaled to this body. The limb word is a REAL limb
# (head|torso|arm|leg), not "bone": the grammar has no `bone` limb, and the
# word is what gives the prim its cluster, i.e. which wounds can expose it.
bones
  ratio 0.38

  # Cranium — a dome, not a scaled face ellipsoid. The face block is the
  # default head, so the dome keeps the zombie's SIZE; only `at` moves, to
  # hold the same 1.6 cm crown-ward offset on a skull bone 0.235 long (the
  # zombie's is 0.16).
  blob head on skull at=0.52 r=0.072 wide=0.88 tall=0.95 deep=0.86

  # Ribcage — the same broad shallow plate as the zombie's, scaled to a
  # narrow chest: the biggest chest blob was r=0.071 when this was sized (a bar
  # from 0.068 tapering to 0.056 since the 2026-10-01 rebuild; the ratio holds)
  # against the zombie's torso 0.150, so r scales by the same ratio
  # (0.052 -> 0.025). Rides the `chest` bone — this torso is a three-bone
  # chain (pelvis/spine1/chest) and the chest IS the ribcage; the gut below
  # keeps its auto-derived bone.
  bar torso on chest from=0.20 to=0.92 r=0.025 wide=1.45 deep=0.55

# The head itself is facePrims(), not geometry authored here — .blob gets the
# zombie's parameterised skull whatever character it is. Pushed toward goblin
# proportions: a bigger cranium relative to a 1.15 m body, a jutting jaw for the
# snout, and a heavier brow for the scowl. The pointed cranium taper and the
# tusks in the WAM file have no analog at all.
face
  # THESE ARE DEFAULT_FACE's VALUES, ON PURPOSE.
  #
  # Until 2026-08-22 the lab discarded a .blob's own face block and built every
  # character from DEFAULT_FACE — so this goblin has ALWAYS rendered as the
  # gaunt default head, and every by-eye decision on it (the hooked nose's
  # reach, the lips' height, the ears, the sunglasses' fit, the whole kit) was
  # tuned against that head while this block sat unread.
  #
  # Wiring the block up correctly therefore CHANGED the goblin: the authored
  # values are a wider, rounder, shallower-crowned skull (headWidth 0.96 vs
  # 0.76, headHeight 1.02 vs 1.161), and under them the nose that reads as a
  # sharp hook becomes a soft bump on a bloated face — it went from 57 mm proud
  # of the cranium to 44 mm, on a head 26% wider.
  #
  # So the block is pinned to what this character has actually been reviewed
  # and approved as. Re-shaping the goblin's skull is a real art decision and
  # it drags the nose, lips and the entire kit fit with it; it should be done
  # deliberately, not as a side effect of a bug fix.
  headRadius 0.118
  headWidth  0.76
  headHeight 1.161
  headDepth  0.894
  jawWidth   0.748
  # EXPRESSION PASS (2026-10-13): the jaw trio below is NOT default any more. The face block's jaw ellipsoid used to run from the
  # cheeks down to the chin as one rigid mass; the mouth has to hinge, so this ellipsoid is SHORTENED FROM BELOW (jawHeight 0.861
  # to 0.537) and the chin is a separate mass `on jaw` in the body block. Centre (0, 1.1496, 0.0487), semi-height 0.049: bottom
  # y 1.100 on the axis, curving up to about y 1.115 at the front, so the lips' corner of the face still hinges. Drop and jut stay
  # within 2-5 mm of the old 0.076 / 0.04 ON PURPOSE: a first plan (jawHeight 0.571, jawDrop 0.049, jawJut 0.073) ended the mass
  # at y 1.122 but had to push it 33 mm forward to keep the mouth line's reach, and that filled the gap under the nose solid
  # (front silhouette +40 mm at y 1.155-1.175: the snout the lips' comment warns about). The top drops to 1.199, inside the
  # cranium. The rest silhouette is pinned by goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts ("head rest profile").
  # TASK 3b (2026-10-14) shortened it again, 0.537 -> 0.467 and drop 0.0743 -> 0.0589: centre (0, 1.165, 0.0487), semi-height
  # 0.043, so its underside clears y 1.132 everywhere in front of z 0.10 and the mouth can open at the lip line. The front just
  # above the seam that it gave up is the upper ridge's (body block).
  jawHeight  0.467
  jawDrop    0.0589
  jawJut     0.045
  noseLength 0.007
  noseWidth  0.379
  noseDrop   0.009
  browHeavy  0.008
  browRise   0.045
  headBlend  0.006
  # THE EYE AREA MOVES (Task 15, owner on the grin clip: "smiling involves also movement of the eye area where the eyes would
  # have to get smaller with the cheeks and eyelids closing more"). These two prims are GENERATED (face.ts facePrims), so their
  # morphs live here, `morph <tag> <channel>:<terms>` (morph.ts terms; never packed: weights 0 change nothing). The painted eye's
  # narrowing is the sheet's (expression.ts `squint`); this is the flesh around it.
  #  - jaw (the face block's jaw ellipsoid: the cheeks under the eyes and the upper muzzle): on squint the cheeks RISE and fill,
  #    up 3 mm with its height x1.07 (the top climbs 6 mm toward the eyes, the bottom at the lip line stays) and 4% wider.
  #  - brow (the ridge over the eyes): down 1.5 mm on squint. Its browDown is TASK 16's (below).
  # TASK 16 (owner: "although his 'eye brows' move his actual brow shape doesnt"; Task 15 moved it 3 mm a unit, linear). The ridge
  # is ONE ellipsoid across both eyes, so its morphs are one-sided (morph.ts `browDown+`/`browDown-`): the two signs want different
  # shapes and only one of them may grow a chord.
  #  - browDown+ (grin 0.8, scowl 1, shout 0.5): the point ellipsoid becomes a 2.4 cm bar along x (move -12 mm, tip +24 mm) bent
  #    DOWN 16 mm and forward 6 at the middle (bend: the middle dips 8 mm under the ends, a V that knits into the nose), the whole
  #    ridge 7 mm lower and 1 mm forward, x-scale x0.78 so the bar is no wider than the old ellipsoid, a touch taller (x1.1), and
  #    its blend x1.6 so the bar's blunt ends melt into the temples. (The dark outline round the dropped ridge in the first frames
  #    was NOT this shape: it was the skin noise's rest anchor jumping at the moved prim's edge; rig-bind.ts morphedRest fixed it.)
  #    Ridge line (the face's most forward point over the eye) at browDown 1: 15 mm lower by the nose (x 0.015), 7.5 mm at the
  #    outer end (x 0.045), 5-6 mm further forward; surprise lifts it 4 mm, so 19 / 11.5 mm of travel between the two
  #    (goblin-cinematic-blob.test.ts pins the travel, the V and the overhang).
  #  - browDown- (surprise -1, smile -0.2): up 5 mm, back 1.5, 15% thinner, blend x2.5 (a softer top edge into the forehead).
  #  Frames: docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/polish/ (eyes-before-after.png).
  # TASK 18: the cheek mass also rises on the SMILE (up 6 mm, forward 2, 6% wider, 8% less tall: its underside climbs ~9 mm, the
  # cheeks bunch into the eyes), and browDown+ is heavier (10 mm lower, a 12 mm V, 25% taller) for the reference's overhanging V.
  # TASK 19 (the evil grin's furrow): `furrow` dips the V's middle 7 mm more and narrows the bar 8%, so the brows angle down and in
  # toward the nose (the inner brow 8 mm lower and 10 mm forward at the evil grin's peak); the inner-brow bulge pair in the body
  # block stands on it. Frames: docs/dev-notes/2026-10-13-goblin-expressions/evil-grin/.
  # TASK 20 (push the evil grin further): the furrow now also drops the whole ridge 3 mm, dips the V 11 mm (was 7) and narrows the
  # bar 12% (was 8%): lower, more knitted brows. The cheek mass is 3% NARROWER on the smile (was 6% wider): less chipmunk.
  morph jaw squint:(0,0.005,0.0015)*(1.05,1.12,1.03) smile:(0,0.006,0.002)*(0.97,0.92,1)
  morph brow browDown+:(-0.014,-0.010,0.002)tip(0.028,0,0)bend(0,-0.024,0.008)*(0.8,1.25,1.05)blend*1.6 browDown-:(0,0.005,-0.0015)*(1,0.85,1)blend*2.5 squint:(0,-0.0015,0) furrow:(0,-0.003,0.001)bend(0,-0.022,0.002)*(0.88,1.05,1)

# The goblin's own face, generated rather than inherited.
#
# Before this, every .blob character wore /assets/lab/zombie-face.png, because
# a shared 64x64 greyscale sheet was the only way to have a face at all — which
# is most of why this thing still read as "the zombie with different limbs".
# The sheet is drawn from these numbers instead: features are DARKENINGS of a
# grainy base, because the shader uses the sheet as an albedo multiplier.
#
# Small close-set eyes under a heavy slanted brow, and a wide down-turned mouth
# — a scowl is mostly brow angle and mouth curve, not eye shape.
sheet
  eyeGap     0.44
  eyeSize    0.070
  eyeRise    0.44
  eyeTilt    16
  eyeSquash  1.70
  # 0, not a pupil: a pupil darkens the very pixels the glow keys off, so it
  # trades a pupil for a dimmer eye. See blob-face-sheet.ts.
  eyePupil   0
  browHeavy  0.90
  browAngle  24
  mouthWidth 0.40
  # The lips are GEOMETRY, and the painted mouth has to land on them or the head
  # wears two mouths. TASK 3b put it ON THE REAL SEAM: the sheet maps face-space
  # y to world y through the cranium (headShape; faceProj 0.58/0.56), so rise
  # 0.805 with curve -0.15 puts the centre at y 1.1335 (the seam) and the
  # corners 4 mm higher; width 0.40 is the opening's +-0.04 m. It used to be a
  # 0.54-wide smile arc centred 5 mm under the seam with corners 6 mm over it.
  mouthRise  0.805
  mouthCurve -0.15
  # A SEAM, not a slab: +-7 mm (was +-1 cm). Paint never darkens the teeth or the
  # inside (a coloured prim replaces the sheet), only the lips around them.
  mouthOpen  0.030
  nostril    0.45
  noseRidge  1.00
  noseWide   0.062
  noseLong   0.235
  jawShade   0.55
  grain      0.10
  seed       5

# The goblin's own flesh, rather than the lab's default preset.
#
# Before this block existed every .blob character wore whichever FLESH_PRESET
# the panel had selected — one global object — so the whole cast rendered as
# the same pink creature in different shapes, which is most of why the first
# goblin read as "the zombie with different limbs" even after the skeleton was
# entirely rebuilt. Colour does more work than proportion here.
#
# These are the field names of FleshMaterial (material.ts), not friendlier
# aliases, for the reason the face block uses FaceParams' own names: an alias
# table is a second list that drifts from the first. Anything not named here
# keeps henenlotter-latex's value — a palette is a partial override, and the
# base is a NAMED preset rather than the panel's current selection so the same
# .blob renders the same in the lab, in a turntable capture and in the game.
#
# Linear RGB. These go through the output sRGB encode on the WebGPU path, which
# lifts the low channels hard — that is why the green is much less saturated
# here than it looks on screen, and why nudging blue up 0.02 does more than it
# reads like it should.
palette
  baseColor     0.34 0.44 0.19
  # Wounds stay red. A green creature bleeding green reads as a plant.
  deepColor     0.46 0.09 0.09
  charColor     0.06 0.07 0.05
  # Clammy rather than latex-wet: a lower, broader highlight than the zombie's.
  specIntensity 0.52
  specRoughness 0.42
  fresnelBoost  0.45
  translucency  0.30
  wetness       0.55
  # THE ALBEDO MOTTLE. surfaceNoiseAmp already roughens the NORMAL, which reads
  # as texture and never as colour, so under this lab's single broad key the
  # creature was one flat tone whichever way it turned. This is the colour
  # twin: fbm in REST space, so the blotches ride a limb through gait instead
  # of swimming over it. Off (0) in every preset, because switching it on
  # changes how a body looks and that is a per-character decision.
  mottleAmp     0.65
  # Low frequency on purpose — patches a hand-span across, not freckles. This
  # is NOT cycles per metre: the shader's fbm multiplies its own input by 4 and
  # 9 for its two octaves, so the number here is about a quarter of the
  # frequency it looks like. The first pass used 5 and produced fine speckle
  # that read as image noise; by ~10 it aliases outright.
  mottleScale   1.6
  # A browner, darker green than the base. Mottling toward something far from
  # the base colour reads as dirt ON the creature rather than variation IN it.
  mottleColor   0.21 0.19 0.06
  # Skin texture: the body's micro-detail normal noise (fbm in rest space,
  # features ~5-11 mm), raised from the inherited 0.06 (about 1 degree of tilt,
  # ten times weaker than the face sheet's grain bump) to the wet-meat preset's
  # 0.22. Owner, 2026-10-01: give the body the face's texture. This is the
  # quick preview; it is bump only, so it reads as a pebbled skin rather than
  # the face's speckle. A per-character `grain` (colour + bump, like the face)
  # is the proper version, below. Owner, 2026-10-02: keep 0.22 with the grain
  # ("fine for now"); 0.06 reads a cleaner speckle if the two ever stack too
  # much (docs/dev-notes/2026-10-02-body-grain/, frame torso-on-n06).
  surfaceNoiseAmp 0.22
  # BODY GRAIN (body-grain.ts; spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-02-body-grain-design.md):
  # the face sheet's speckle on the rest of the body, in two octaves. Same
  # units as the sheet block's `grain`, and the same value, so face and body
  # carry one contrast: hard-edged rest-space cells, face-sized (3.5 mm) up
  # close and 1.2 cm farther out, each an albedo multiply of up to +-22% and a
  # bump matched to the face's relief. It fades out where the face sheet
  # covers the head; each octave fades as its cells drop below ~1.5 SDF pixels
  # (in the lab turntable the coarse octave is full out to ~1.5 m and gone by
  # ~3 m).
  grain         0.10
